          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What follow-up interval does guidance describe after starting a new blood pressure medicine?",
          "answer": "Guidance in the retrieved material suggests **about monthly review after starting or changing blood pressure treatment, until the target is reached.** Once readings are stable, review every **three to six months**. This comes from the document \"Hypertension: when treatment starts and which options are first-line,\" and the document \"Hypertension: diagnostic thresholds and blood-pressure targets\" gives the same schedule.\n\nFor one class of medicine, the first-line guidance also asks for a check of kidney function and potassium after starting. This applies to agents that act on the renin-angiotensin system.\n\n**Differences between authorities:** The retrieved passages show disagreement about blood pressure thresholds and how aggressively to treat low-risk stage 1 hypertension. They do not show a disagreement about the follow-up interval itself. The sources describe the schedule as coming from \"major cardiology society consensus guidance\" without naming a single body, so I cannot tell you which organization set each interval.\n\n**What the retrieved material does not cover:** It does not describe a home monitoring schedule, and it does not say how strong the evidence is behind the monthly interval. I can't judge that from these passages.\n\nA clinician can tell you how often you specifically should be checked, based on your own readings and health history.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
